<title>UK: Goldman group to buy UK gov't buildings - paper.</title>
<headline>Goldman group to buy UK gov't buildings - paper.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1997-08-01</dateline>
<text>
<p>A consortium led by U.S. investment bank  Goldman Sachs is to buy the entire property portfolio of a British  government department for 400 million stg, The Times reported on Friday.</p>
<p>The report said the Partnership Property Management (PPM) consortium  had been selected from a shortlist of two to acquire the property  owned by the Department of Social Security (DSS).</p>
<p>PPM comprises the Whitehall Street Real Estate Partnership, a fund  run by Goldman Sachs, builder Amec Plc, developer Berkeley Group,  Vines Management, Compass Management and surveyors Richard Ellis.</p>
<p>Its rival bidder Mapeley Holdings included Nations Bank and property  company Burford.</p>
<p>PPM will supply the DSS with service accommodation under a 20-year  contract for a fixed fee linked to inflation, the report said.</p>
<p>-- London Newsroom +44 171 542 7717</p>
